---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>An education loan is secured or unsecured depending mostly on how much you borrow.</strong>
    Small loans usually need no security, mid-size loans usually need a guarantor, and large loans usually need property or a fixed deposit. The loan type is one decision. The loan is repaid from your future pay, so the skills and proof you build beside the degree matter as much as the paperwork.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short answer:</strong> under the Indian Banks' Association (IBA) model scheme, which many banks follow, a loan up to ₹4 lakh needs no security, a loan up to ₹7.5 lakh usually needs a guarantor (someone who agrees to repay if you cannot), and a bigger loan needs property or a deposit. Government schemes can remove the security even for bigger loans, and each bank sets its own rules, so confirm the band in the sanction letter.</li>
      <li><strong>Decision filter:</strong> "unsecured" does not mean nobody is at risk. Your co-borrower (usually a parent, who signs the loan with you) can be asked for the full debt, and a secured loan puts the pledged asset on the line as well.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> check PM-Vidyalaxmi first if your institution is on its list, then get one secured and one unsecured quote and compare the rupee gap. Borrow the smallest amount that closes the real gap, because the EMI will be paid from your own pay.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p class="more-guides">
    Families often meet this question with an admission letter in one hand and a request for property papers in the other. If a loan of this size worries you, <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-college-students/">career counselling for college students</a> helps you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities by checking the course, the loan and the skill plan together. Related money decisions sit in <a href="/blog/college-degrees/">College Degrees</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <p class="note-line">General information, not financial advice; get the terms in writing. No agent can promise that a loan will be approved, so never pay one for a guarantee. Figures were checked on 16 November 2026.</p>

  <h2 id="answer">The short answer: the amount you borrow decides if an education loan is secured or unsecured</h2>

  <p>
    A loan has no fixed label. The bank chooses what to ask for based on the loan size, your institution, your course and who stands behind you.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>secured</strong> loan means you or your family pledge something the bank can sell if repayment fails, such as a house, a fixed deposit or an insurance policy with a surrender value. An <strong>unsecured</strong> loan means nothing is pledged, so the bank decides on your seat, your course and how steadily the co-borrower earns and repays. Security and collateral mean the same thing here.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Two beliefs that cost families money</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"Unsecured means nobody has to repay if things go wrong." It does not. The co-borrower still owes the full amount.</li>
      <li>"Secured is always the better deal because the rate is lower." Sometimes the saving is small next to the risk to the asset.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bands">The three loan-size bands, and what each one asks for</h2>

  <p>
    The Indian Banks' Association (IBA) model scheme is the pattern many public sector banks follow. In 2001 the RBI asked banks to adopt it and said <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=369&Mode=0" rel="noopener" target="_blank">no security may be insisted upon for loans up to ₹4 lakh</a>. A June 2026 report in <a href="https://www.theweek.in/theweek/business/2026/06/20/education-loans-what-you-need-to-know-about-approval-repayment.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Week</a> describes the same three bands as typical today.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Loan size</th>
          <th>Security usually asked</th>
          <th>Who stands behind the loan</th>
          <th>In plain words</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {bandRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Loan size"><strong>{row.size}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Security usually asked">{row.security}</td>
            <td data-label="Who stands behind the loan">{row.behind}</td>
            <td data-label="In plain words">{row.kind}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">Banks apply their own version of these bands, and some private lenders ignore them. Confirm the band in your sanction letter (the bank's written approval with the terms), not in a verbal promise.</p>

  <p>To see what an EMI from any of these bands would take from a first salary, run your numbers through the free <a href="/tools/education-cost-payback-planner/">education cost payback planner</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="meaning">What secured and unsecured mean for you in practice</h2>

  <div class="split-grid">
    <div class="split-card split-card--risk">
      <div class="split-card__label">Secured loan</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The bank holds a claim on your asset until the loan is cleared.</li>
        <li>The rate is often lower, but the gap can be small, as the rupee check below shows.</li>
        <li>The bank values the asset and checks the papers first, which takes time and costs money.</li>
        <li>If repayment fails, the bank can act against the asset.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="split-card split-card--grow">
      <div class="split-card__label">Unsecured loan</div>
      <ul>
        <li>No asset is pledged. The bank judges your admission, institution, course and the co-borrower.</li>
        <li>NBFCs (non-bank lenders) sometimes lend without security, and their rates vary a lot. <a href="https://propelld.com/site/blog/nbfc-education-loan" rel="noopener" target="_blank">One comparison page</a>, updated in November 2026, lists about 9.5% to 19.5% across lenders.</li>
        <li>The co-borrower's credit score matters. Lenders generally prefer 750 or above.</li>
        <li>If repayment fails, no asset is seized, but the lender can still recover the debt.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>Your parent is not a bystander on an unsecured loan. A co-borrower owes the full balance from day one, and the bank can ask them for it.</p>
    <p>A guarantor owes only if the borrowers default, yet the loan can still show on their credit report. A large study-abroad loan is riskier again: repayment may depend on a visa, a job abroad and the exchange rate, so read <a href="/blog/study-abroad/education-loan-for-study-abroad-india/">education loan for study abroad India</a> before you plan around one.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="no-collateral">Large loans that are still collateral-free</h2>

  <p>
    The ₹7.5 lakh line is not a wall. Government-backed routes let some students borrow without pledging property.
  </p>

  <div class="scheme-grid">
    <div class="scheme-card">
      <h3>PM-Vidyalaxmi</h3>
      <p>Students admitted on merit to the 1,425 listed institutions get loans with no collateral and no guarantor. The loan amount follows fees and related costs, and the July 2026 scheme document is the place to confirm any limit.</p>
      <p>Interest is capped at the bank's external benchmark lending rate plus 0.5%. Repayment starts after the moratorium (the course plus one year, with no monthly instalment, or EMI, to pay) and can run up to 15 years.</p>
      <p class="scheme-row scheme-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Management quota and NRI quota seats are excluded. In 2025-26, 70,852 loans were sanctioned against 110,667 applications, so keep a backup plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scheme-card">
      <h3>Interest help</h3>
      <p>This lowers your cost, not the collateral rule. PM-Vidyalaxmi gives 3% interest subvention (the government pays that share of the interest) during the moratorium on loans up to ₹10 lakh, when family income is up to ₹8 lakh.</p>
      <p>A separate central scheme pays the full interest during the moratorium on loans up to ₹10 lakh for technical and professional courses at accredited institutions, when family income is up to ₹4.5 lakh.</p>
      <p class="scheme-row scheme-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Only 1 lakh students a year can get the 3% benefit, and you cannot combine it with another interest subsidy or scholarship.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scheme-card">
      <h3>Credit guarantee</h3>
      <p>The Credit Guarantee Fund Scheme for Education Loans, set up in 2015, covers 75% of a collateral-free loan up to ₹7.5 lakh. It has no family income limit.</p>
      <p class="scheme-row scheme-row--teal"><span>Best for</span>Students who cannot offer collateral or a guarantor and need a loan up to ₹7.5 lakh.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Sources for these figures: the Government of India's <a href="https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2026/jul/doc2026729936401.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">PM-Vidyalaxmi document of 29 July 2026</a> and a <a href="https://www.businesstoday.in/education/story/collateral-free-loans-interest-subsidies-centre-puts-rs-1050-crore-into-education-loan-schemes-546001-2026-07-30" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Business Today report of 30 July 2026</a>. Apply through a bank or the <a href="https://www.vidyalakshmi.co.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Vidya Lakshmi loan portal</a>, and confirm the current list and limits there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="route">Which route to try first</h2>

  <p>
    Start with the route that puts the least family property at risk, and move down only when the one above does not fit. The order follows the risk to what your family owns, not which loan is cheapest.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Your situation</th>
          <th>Try first</th>
          <th>What it puts at risk</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {routeRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Your situation"><strong>{row.situation}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Try first">{row.first}</td>
            <td data-label="What it puts at risk">{row.risk}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    A slightly higher rate is a fair price for keeping the family's property out of the contract, and the next section puts that price in rupees.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">Does a secured loan cost less? A rupee check</h2>

  <p>
    In June 2026 The Week reported SBI at 9.40% with collateral and 9.90% without. Rates float with the market, so read both as the size of a gap, not as today's offer.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p><strong>An illustration, not a real offer.</strong> On ₹10 lakh repaid over ten years, 9.40% gives an EMI near ₹12,885 and about ₹5.46 lakh in total interest. At 9.90%, the EMI is near ₹13,160 and total interest is about ₹5.79 lakh.</p>
    <p>That half-point gap is about ₹33,000 over the whole repayment, and a full-point gap is about ₹66,000. Interest during the moratorium comes on top. If the whole amount were paid out on day one and interest ran as simple interest, five years at 9.40% would add about ₹4.7 lakh, though real loans are paid out in instalments, so the true figure is usually lower.</p>
  </div>

  <p>
    To run these numbers with your own loan, moratorium and payout pattern, use the <a href="/tools/education-loan-emi-calculator/">education loan EMI calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>Size beats type. A smaller unsecured loan puts less at risk than a bigger secured one, and the surest way to cut risk is to need less money.</p>
    <p>Before a family pledges its home to save a fraction of a percent, price the saving in rupees, then see what a lower-fee seat, a scholarship or a government college would do to the amount you must borrow.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="default">What happens if repayment fails</h2>

  <div class="ladder">
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">1</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Missed instalments</div>
        <p>Late charges apply and your credit record starts to suffer. The co-borrower's record is affected too.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">2</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Overdue for 90 days</div>
        <p>The bank classes the account as non-performing, which means it treats the loan as unpaid, and sends notices to you and every co-borrower or guarantor.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">3</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Secured loan: the asset is at risk</div>
        <p>The bank can give a 60-day notice and then take over or sell the pledged property or deposit under the SARFAESI Act, without first going to court. Your loan agreement spells out the steps.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">4</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Unsecured loan: no asset, still a debt</div>
        <p>There is nothing to seize, but the lender can pursue recovery through legal action, and the co-borrower still owes the balance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Speak to the lender before the account turns overdue. Ask about a longer repayment period or a short pause, and know that what is offered depends on the bank.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ask">What to ask the bank before you sign</h2>

  <div class="ask-list">
    {askItems.map((item, index) => (
      <div class="ask-item">
        <div class="ask-item__num">{index + 1}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="ask-item__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money check before you pay for a degree with a loan</h2>

  <p>
    Our strongest suggestion: keep the fee for a course or degree near a tenth of the family's whole education budget, and treat anything above about a fifth as a caution that needs proof for that exact course. This line is for degrees and costly programmes, not for free tutorials or low-priced online courses.
  </p>

  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: take a family whose whole education budget is ₹20 lakh, so the suggested fee is about ₹2 lakh and the caution starts near ₹4 lakh. A degree that needs ₹12 lakh of borrowing sits far above both, so it needs strong proof for this course and college, such as a checked placement median, a licence requirement, or lab and clinical access. Whatever is left should pay for tools, practice and a reserve.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some fields can be learned and shown through free YouTube lessons, good online courses, projects and internships, so compare that route before you borrow. Medicine, nursing and other licensed fields still need the formal degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    A loan that needs the family home as security is a reason to compare a lower-cost college and a skill-first route before you sign. Money kept for internet, tools and projects builds the proof of work that makes repayment realistic. <strong>Build skills and proof of work while you study, because the EMI is paid from your pay and not from the certificate.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Check before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    The four questions test the loan against your life, and the three proofs test it against the real world, so pass both before you sign.
  </p>

  <h3>The four questions</h3>

  <div class="protocol">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="protocol-row">
        <div class="protocol-row__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div>
